//? Title: A* Search: Walking Over a Volcano
//? Subtitle: Optimal path planning on the Maunga Whau elevation map with datamunge's algorithms::terrain_a_star, with heuristics derived from the cost model
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A* finds a cheapest path by expanding nodes in order of \(f=g+h\): the cost \(g\) of reaching
a node plus a heuristic estimate \(h\) of the cost still to come. We prove that an *admissible*
\(h\), one that never overestimates, makes the first path to reach the goal optimal. We also
show that A* must expand every node with \(g^*+h<C^*\) and never one with \(g^*+h>C^*\), so a
larger admissible heuristic can only save work. The test problem is a walk across Maunga
Whau (Mt Eden), using R's `volcano` elevation grid, now `dm::datasets::volcano()`. Each step
costs its length plus 8.3 metres for every metre climbed, which is Naismith's rule for hill
walking. The cheapest route from the south-east foot to the north-west foot avoids the summit
and climbs only 17 m. We derive three admissible heuristics from the cost model.
Dijkstra's algorithm (\(h=0\)) expands 4,443 of the 5,307 cells and the best heuristic
1,732, and every count lies within the bounds the theory predicts. Weighted A* with \(f=g+wh\)
expands fewer cells still, and its path never costs more than \(w\) times the optimum.
)

\toc

> The algorithm and why it is optimal

Let a graph have non-negative step costs \(c(u,v)\), a start \(s\) and a goal \(t\). Write
\(g^*(v)\) for the cost of the cheapest path from \(s\) to \(v\), \(h^*(v)\) for the cheapest
cost from \(v\) to \(t\), and \(C^*=g^*(t)\) for the optimum. A* keeps a priority queue of
*open* nodes. For each it stores the best known cost \(g(v)\) and the priority
\(f(v)=g(v)+h(v)\). It repeatedly removes the open node with the smallest \(f\), stops if that
node is the goal, and otherwise *relaxes* its neighbours: if \(g(u)+c(u,v)<g(v)\), it records
the cheaper route and puts \(v\) back on the queue.

```
g[s] = 0; open = {s}
loop:
    u = open node with the smallest g[u] + h(u)
    if u == t: return the path to u via the parent pointers
    for each neighbour v of u:
        if g[u] + c(u,v) < g[v]: g[v] = g[u] + c(u,v); parent[v] = u; open.push(v)
```

With \(h=0\) this is Dijkstra's algorithm, which expands nodes in order of distance from the
start, in every direction. The heuristic tilts the search toward the goal.

>> Admissible heuristics give optimal paths

Call \(h\) *admissible* if \(h(v)\le h^*(v)\) for every \(v\), and \(h(t)=0\). Suppose A* is
about to return the goal with cost \(g(t)>C^*\). Take an optimal path
\(s=v_0,v_1,\dots,v_k=t\). Its start is closed and its end is not yet expanded, so it has a
first node \(v_i\) still open. All its predecessors on the path were expanded with their
optimal costs, so \(g(v_i)=g^*(v_i)\). Then

$$
f(v_i)=g^*(v_i)+h(v_i)\le g^*(v_i)+h^*(v_i)=C^*<g(t)=f(t),
$$
\alttext(f of v i equals g star of v i plus h of v i, which is at most g star of v i plus h star of v i, which equals C star, which is less than g of t, which equals f of t.)

so A* would remove \(v_i\) before \(t\), a contradiction. Hence the returned path is optimal.
The argument only needs \(h\) to never overestimate on optimal paths.

>> Consistent heuristics, and which nodes get expanded

Call \(h\) *consistent* if \(h(u)\le c(u,v)+h(v)\) for every step: the estimate cannot drop by
more than the step costs. With \(h(t)=0\), summing this along any path shows consistency
implies admissibility. It also makes \(f\) non-decreasing along every path, because
\(f(v)=g(u)+c(u,v)+h(v)\ge g(u)+h(u)=f(u)\). So A* removes nodes in non-decreasing order of
\(f\), each node's first removal already has \(g=g^*\), and no node is ever expanded twice. Two
consequences pin down the work exactly:

$$
\{\,v : g^*(v)+h(v)<C^*\,\}\;\subseteq\;\text{expanded}\;\subseteq\;\{\,v : g^*(v)+h(v)\le C^*\,\}.
$$
\alttext(The set of v with g star of v plus h of v less than C star is contained in the expanded set, which is contained in the set of v with g star of v plus h of v at most C star.)

A node with \(f^*<C^*\) must come out of the queue before the goal, and one with \(f^*>C^*\)
never does. Only ties at exactly \(C^*\) depend on the tie-breaking rule. So if
\(h_1\le h_2\) are both consistent, every node that \(h_2\) must expand is also expanded by
\(h_1\): the larger heuristic *dominates*. The ideal is \(h=h^*\), which expands only nodes on
optimal paths.

>> Weighted A*: trading optimality for speed

Using \(f=g+w\,h\) with \(w>1\) pushes the search harder toward the goal. The argument above
still bounds the result. When the goal is removed with cost \(g(t)\), the first open node
\(v_i\) on an optimal path satisfies

$$
g(t)=f(t)\le f(v_i)=g^*(v_i)+w\,h(v_i)\le w\bigl(g^*(v_i)+h^*(v_i)\bigr)=w\,C^*,
$$
\alttext(g of t equals f of t, which is at most f of v i, which equals g star of v i plus w h of v i, which is at most w times g star of v i plus h star of v i, which equals w C star.)

so weighted A* returns a path at most \(w\) times as costly as the best, often after far fewer
expansions.

> The terrain

`dm::datasets::volcano()` is R's `volcano`: heights of Maunga Whau, a volcanic cone in
Auckland, on an \(87\times61\) grid with 10 m spacing. `dm::algorithms::terrain_from_frame()`
turns the long-format table into a `TerrainGrid`. Each cell is a node with up to 8
neighbours. The walker starts near the south-east foot and must reach the north-west foot,
on opposite sides of the cone.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/a_star_terrain.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto volcano = dm::datasets::volcano();
  std::print("{}\n\n", volcano | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("cells"), dm::agg::min("elevation", "lowest (m)"),
                                               dm::agg::median("elevation", "median (m)"),
                                               dm::agg::max("elevation", "highest (m)"), dm::agg::max("x", "x extent (m)"),
                                               dm::agg::max("y", "y extent (m)"))
                           | dm::to_md(0));

  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(volcano, "x", "y", "elevation");
  const std::size_t start = 80 * grid.cols + 5, goal = 5 * grid.cols + 55;
  auto xy = [&](std::size_t cell) {
    return std::pair{grid.cell_size * (cell / grid.cols), grid.cell_size * (cell % grid.cols)};
  };
  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.rows; ++i) xs.push_back(grid.cell_size * i);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.cols; ++j) ys.push_back(grid.cell_size * j);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(grid.rows, std::vector<double>(grid.cols));
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < grid.elevation.size(); ++c) z[c / grid.cols][c % grid.cols] = grid.elevation[c];
  const auto [sx, sy] = xy(start);
  const auto [gx, gy] = xy(goal);
  std::print("Grid {} x {} cells of {} m. Start ({}, {}) at {} m, goal ({}, {}) at {} m. "
             "Straight-line distance {:.0f} m.\n",
             grid.rows, grid.cols, grid.cell_size, sx, sy, grid.elevation[start], gx, gy, grid.elevation[goal],
             std::hypot(gx - sx, gy - sy));

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::terrain_colors(20))
      .points({sx}, {sy}, "start", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 9.0)
      .points({gx}, {gy}, "goal", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 9.0)
      .text(sx, sy + 35, "start")
      .text(gx, gy - 35, "goal")
      .title("Maunga Whau elevation (m): 94 to 195")
      .x_label("x (m), rows of R's volcano")
      .y_label("y (m)")
      .size(900, 660)
      .save_svg("build/a_star_terrain.svg");
}
```
