| octile + climb | 0.858 | 0.881 | 0.654 | 1.000 | 827 |
\image(figures/a_star_tightness.svg)
// result_end

\caption(\(h/h^*\) for every cell against its elevation, for the three non-zero heuristics. A value of 1 means the heuristic equals the true remaining cost.)
\alttext(Three scatter plots of h over h star, from 0 to 1.05, against cell elevation from 94 to 195 metres, for the Euclidean, octile and octile plus climb heuristics. All three have values between about 0.52 and 1, the lowest for cells near 150 metres. Many cells sit at exactly 1 across all elevations, most of all for octile plus climb. For low cells below about 105 metres, octile plus climb lies between 0.8 and 1, higher than the other two.)

The heuristics see between half and all of the remaining cost. On average the straight-line
distance captures 80%, the octile distance 84% and octile plus climb 86%. They agree exactly
with \(h^*\) for 50, 750 and 827 cells respectively: cells from which the cheapest route has
the shortest possible length and no climbing beyond what the heuristic counts. The worst
ratios, near 0.52, are in the crater at about 150 m. A walker there must first climb out over
the rim, and no heuristic built from distance and net height can see that. The climb term
helps most on low ground below the goal's 108 m, where it adds the unavoidable ascent.

> How much should climbing cost?

The route depends on \(\lambda\). With \(\lambda=0\) only distance matters and the walker goes
over the top. As \(\lambda\) grows, longer routes that avoid climbing become cheaper. We
replan with several values of \(\lambda\) and compare the routes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/a_star_climb.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto volcano = dm::datasets::volcano();
  const std::vector<double> lambdas = {0.0, 2.0, 5000.0 / 600.0, 20.0};
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colours = {{17, 24, 39}, {37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {124, 58, 237}};

  auto base = alg::terrain_from_frame(volcano, "x", "y", "elevation");
  const std::size_t start = 80 * base.cols + 5, goal = 5 * base.cols + 55;
  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < base.rows; ++i) xs.push_back(base.cell_size * i);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < base.cols; ++j) ys.push_back(base.cell_size * j);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(base.rows, std::vector<double>(base.cols));
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < base.elevation.size(); ++c) z[c / base.cols][c % base.cols] = base.elevation[c];
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create().image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::terrain_colors(20));

  std::print("| \\(\\lambda\\) | length (m) | ascent (m) | descent (m) | highest point (m) | cost | "
             "Naismith time (min) | expanded |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < lambdas.size(); ++k) {
    auto grid = base;
    grid.climb_cost = lambdas[k];
    const auto path = alg::terrain_a_star(grid, start, goal, alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb);
    double top = 0.0;
    std::vector<double> px, py;
    for (std::size_t c : path.path) {
      top = std::max(top, grid.elevation[c]);
      px.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c / grid.cols));
      py.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c % grid.cols));
    }
    const double minutes = 60.0 * (path.length / 5000.0 + path.ascent / 600.0);
    std::print("| {:.2f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.1f} | {} |\n", lambdas[k], path.length,
               path.ascent, path.descent, top, path.cost, minutes, path.expansion_order.size());
    plot.lines(px, py, "", colours[k], 3.0);
    names.push_back(std::format("lambda = {:.2f}", lambdas[k]));
  }
  plot.legend(names, colours)
      .y_limits(-20, 800)
      .title("Cheapest routes for four climbing penalties")
      .x_label("x (m)")
      .y_label("y (m)")
      .size(900, 660)
      .save_svg("build/a_star_climb.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(\lambda\) | length (m) | ascent (m) | descent (m) | highest point (m) | cost | Naismith time (min) | expanded |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.00 | 957 | 121 | 115 | 189 | 957 | 23.6 | 226 |
| 2.00 | 957 | 64 | 58 | 150 | 1085 | 17.9 | 1612 |
| 8.33 | 1150 | 17 | 11 | 117 | 1291 | 15.5 | 1732 |
| 20.00 | 1236 | 11 | 5 | 110 | 1456 | 15.9 | 1442 |
\image(figures/a_star_climb.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The optimal route for each climbing penalty \(\lambda\), drawn over the elevation map.)
\alttext(The elevation map with four routes from the start at the bottom right to the goal at the top left. Lambda 0, black: goes west along y equals 80, then straight north-west across the cone's southern shoulder near the summit. Lambda 2, blue: a straight diagonal north-west over the eastern ridge, then west along y equals 540. Lambda 8.33, red: north up the eastern side to y equals 300, then north-west to the top edge and west along the northern edge of the map. Lambda 20, purple: like the red route but swings further east, to x equals 860, before turning north-west.)

With no penalty for climbing (\(\lambda=0\)) the route is as short as the grid allows, 957 m,
and crosses the cone's shoulder at 189 m, with 121 m of ascent. Many routes share that length
on an 8-connected grid, and A* returns one of them. With \(\lambda=2\) it picks another route
of the same length that crosses the lower eastern ridge instead, halving the ascent to 64 m.
With Naismith's \(\lambda=8.33\) the walker gives up 193 m of extra distance to avoid all but
17 m of climbing, and goes round the north of the cone at no more than 117 m. With
\(\lambda=20\) it swings further out still, to save 6 more metres of climb.

The Naismith time, an hour per 5 km plus an hour per 600 m of ascent, is exactly the cost at
\(\lambda=8.33\) converted to minutes, so that route is the fastest of the four: 15.5
minutes, against 23.6 for the shortest route over the top.

> Weighted A*

Finally we trade optimality for speed with \(f=g+w\,h\), using the octile-plus-climb heuristic
and Naismith's \(\lambda\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/a_star_weighted.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(dm::datasets::volcano(), "x", "y", "elevation", 5000.0 / 600.0);
  const std::size_t start = 80 * grid.cols + 5, goal = 5 * grid.cols + 55;
  const auto best = alg::terrain_a_star(grid, start, goal, alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb);

  std::print("| \\(w\\) | expanded (reopening) | cost / \\(C^*\\) (reopening) | expanded (no reopening) | "
             "cost / \\(C^*\\) (no reopening) | length (m) | ascent (m) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> ws, expanded_reopen, expanded_closed, ratio_reopen, ratio_closed;
  for (double w : {1.0, 1.1, 1.25, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0, 10.0}) {
    const auto again = alg::terrain_a_star(grid, start, goal, alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb, w, true);
    const auto once = alg::terrain_a_star(grid, start, goal, alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb, w, false);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n", w, again.expansion_order.size(),
               again.cost / best.cost, once.expansion_order.size(), once.cost / best.cost, once.length, once.ascent);
    ws.push_back(w);
    expanded_reopen.push_back(static_cast<double>(again.expansion_order.size()));
    expanded_closed.push_back(static_cast<double>(once.expansion_order.size()));
    ratio_reopen.push_back(again.cost / best.cost);
    ratio_closed.push_back(once.cost / best.cost);
  }

  const dm::RGB blue{37, 99, 235}, orange{245, 158, 11};
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(ws, expanded_reopen, "", orange, 2.0)
                 .points(ws, expanded_reopen, "", orange, 6.0)
                 .lines(ws, expanded_closed, "", blue, 2.0)
                 .points(ws, expanded_closed, "", blue, 6.0)
                 .abline_h(static_cast<double>(grid.elevation.size()), dm::RGB{156, 163, 175}, 1.0)
                 .legend({"with reopening", "without reopening"}, {orange, blue})
                 .y_limits(0, 7000)
                 .title("Cells expanded")
                 .x_label("weight w")
                 .y_label("expansions"));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(ws, ratio_reopen, "", orange, 2.0)
                 .points(ws, ratio_reopen, "", orange, 6.0)
                 .lines(ws, ratio_closed, "", blue, 2.0)
                 .points(ws, ratio_closed, "", blue, 6.0)
                 .y_limits(0.95, 2.0)
                 .title("Path cost relative to the optimum")
                 .x_label("weight w")
                 .y_label("cost / C*"));
  layout.size(1200, 440).save_svg("build/a_star_weighted.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(w\) | expanded (reopening) | cost / \(C^*\) (reopening) | expanded (no reopening) | cost / \(C^*\) (no reopening) | length (m) | ascent (m) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1732 | 1.000 | 1732 | 1.000 | 1150 | 17 |
| 1.1 | 1483 | 1.000 | 1298 | 1.003 | 1153 | 17 |
| 1.25 | 1260 | 1.009 | 842 | 1.029 | 1145 | 22 |
| 1.5 | 1318 | 1.039 | 752 | 1.039 | 1133 | 25 |
| 2 | 5391 | 1.086 | 934 | 1.079 | 1119 | 33 |
| 3 | 645 | 1.484 | 397 | 1.496 | 1024 | 109 |
| 5 | 79 | 1.520 | 79 | 1.520 | 963 | 120 |
| 10 | 76 | 1.167 | 76 | 1.167 | 957 | 66 |
\image(figures/a_star_weighted.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Weighted A* with the octile-plus-climb heuristic, with and without reopening closed cells. Left: expansions, with the grid's 5,307 cells in grey. Right: the path's cost as a multiple of the optimum, which the theory bounds by \(w\).)
\alttext(Two line plots against weight w from 1 to 10. Left, expansions: without reopening (blue) the count falls from 1,732 at w equals 1 to 752 at w equals 1.5, rises to 934 at w equals 2, then falls to about 80 for w of 5 and 10. With reopening (orange) it follows a similar course except for a spike to 5,391 at w equals 2, above the grey line at the grid size of 5,307. Right, path cost over the optimum: both curves rise from 1.0 to about 1.08 at w equals 2 and 1.5 at w equals 3 and 5, then fall to 1.17 at w equals 10.)

A small weight buys a lot. With \(w=1.25\) and no reopening, A* expands 842 cells, less than
half of the 1,732 for plain A*, and returns a path 2.9% more expensive than the optimum. At
\(w=1.5\) it expands 752 cells for a path 3.9% dearer. Every path stays far inside the
guarantee: the worst ratio is 1.52, at \(w=5\). Large weights make the search almost greedy.
With \(w\ge5\) it expands under 80 cells and heads straight for the goal over the top. That
costs about 50% more at \(w=5\), while \(w=10\) happens to find a better compromise route,
17% over. Neither expansions nor cost need change monotonically with \(w\).

Reopening makes things worse here. \(w\,h\) is not consistent, so cheaper routes to closed
cells keep turning up. With reopening, \(w=2\) expands 5,391 entries, more than the grid has
cells, for a path no better than the one found without reopening. The bound
\(\text{cost}\le w\,C^*\) also holds without reopening (Likhachev, Gordon and Thrun, 2003), so
not reopening is the usual choice for weighted A*.

> Summary

- A* with an admissible heuristic returns an optimal path. With a consistent heuristic it
  expands every cell with \(g^*+h<C^*\) and none with \(g^*+h>C^*\). On the volcano all four
  heuristics found the optimum, \(C^*=1291.5\), and their expanded counts fell inside these
  bounds.
- Heuristics derived from the cost model are larger and so save more work. Octile distance
  plus the unavoidable climb expanded 1,732 cells, against 4,443 for Dijkstra and 2,289 for
  the straight-line distance.
- The cost model decides the route. With Naismith's rule the cheapest crossing goes round the
  cone: 1,150 m with 17 m of climbing, against 957 m with 121 m over the top, and it is the
  fastest walk.
- Weighted A* with \(w=1.25\) to \(1.5\) halves the work for paths within 4% of optimal,
  well inside the guarantee \(w\,C^*\). Without reopening it is both faster and no worse.
- Floating-point ties can break the textbook guarantee that a consistent heuristic never
  reopens a node. Improvements must exceed a small tolerance.

>> Complexity and relatives

With a binary heap, A* costs \(O((V+E)\log V)\) in the worst case, the same as Dijkstra, and
\(O(V)\) memory for the costs and parent pointers. The heuristic decides how much of that worst
case is paid. Memory, not time, is usually the limit on large graphs, which motivates
*iterative-deepening A** (IDA*), which uses linear memory by repeating depth-first searches
with an increasing \(f\) bound. *Jump point search* prunes symmetric paths on uniform-cost
grids. *Contraction hierarchies* and *landmark (ALT)* heuristics, which use the triangle
inequality with precomputed distances to a few landmarks, make road-network routing fast.
`dm::algorithms::a_star_search()` runs A* on any graph given as an edge list with a
per-vertex heuristic; `terrain_edges()` turns a terrain grid into that form.
